export const essays = {
  "guides/french-bulldog-health-issues.html": `<p>Snoring at 2 a.m. is the opening item on the problem list when a French Bulldog hits the exam table. Brachycephalic obstructive airway syndrome here comes from narrow nostrils and a long soft palate. In the living room that dog breathes louder against a cushion, fades on a mild block, or sleeps with the chin propped up to pull air. Gums that go dusky, a collapse after play, or choking sounds after midnight belong in the clinic before the night is over. Ask for a breathing grade while the patient is awake and quiet, and ask again if the noise deepens. Heat piles onto that airway. Panting is inefficient on this skull, so a warm afternoon can turn a loud breather into a heatstroke emergency. Cool rooms, and a harness sitting on the chest, are already in the house kit.</p>
<p>The spine is the next file in the workup. Hemivertebrae are the malformed spinal bones this breed carries, and intervertebral disc disease shows up as back pain, a wobble, or a dog who stops climbing. Every flight of stairs is a carry. Jumping off the sofa is the move to block with a low ramp. What people notice first is often a yelp when lifted, a tight abdomen, or hind feet that scuff. Spinal imaging and a neurologic look belong on the list when those signs show. Parent dogs in a breeding conversation should already have a spine comment beside the rest of the screen to list below.</p>
<p>Skin fails inside creases. Facial-fold dermatitis and tail-pocket dermatitis are moisture diseases. Wipe the facial crease and the pocket under the tail, then dry both, because a damp groove turns red and starts to smell. Allergies sit next to that job. Paw licking, neck scratching, and an ear flare all feed the same inflamed skin, and the fold looks worse on itchy weeks. Bring a photo of a calm day so the doctor can compare color and odor. Food trials and flea control are appointment questions, handled with the record open at the visit.</p>
<p>Many litters need a cesarean. Talking with a breeder, ask how the puppies arrived and whether the dam went to surgery. That answer belongs in the whelping plan and in the money set aside for the next litter. AKC caps the breed at not over 28 pounds, and the adults to weigh usually land from 16 to 28 pounds. Shoulder height is 11 to 13 inches, while the bat ears make the head look taller than the withers. The published span runs 10 to 12 years. Pounds stacked onto that small frame make the airway and the back work harder, so the scale is part of the medical plan, right beside the drugs.</p>
<p>Screening often includes patellas, spine, heart, eyes, and a breathing grade. Those belongs five named before trusting a litter. Put a recheck on the calendar at age 3 and another at age 5, even when the dog seems easy, because the palate, the discs, and the skin do not send reminders. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/french-bulldog">VCA French Bulldog clinical notes</a> walk through the same cluster to keep seeing: airway, spine, skin, and the whelping reality of this compact dog.</p>`,
  "guides/french-bulldog-temperament.html": `<p>Bat ears swivel before the feet move. The personality is a companion one: they track the room, they want a lap, and they announce a knock with a whole-body wiggle. AKC recognized the breed in 1898, and the dog in the kitchen still behaves like a parlor clown who expects to be included in every conversation. The short smooth coat and the upright ears make every mood easy to read. A happy dog looks bigger. A worried dog plants and watches.</p>
<p>Play arrives in bursts. This dog will shove a toy into a hand, sprint the hallway, then drop into a heap and snore. Cap the roughhousing. Wrestling gets 3 minutes, and by minute 5 trade the game for a chew on the rug. The body sets that limit. A warm room shortens the fun, and a stubborn streak shows up the moment the harness is asked to leave a smell on the sidewalk. These dogs are affectionate and also opinionated. They will sit on a foot until the plan changes to one they like.</p>
<p>Strangers usually get a greeting, then a decision to stay close to their person. Children do well when the child sits and lets the dog climb up, because the sturdy chest likes contact and the back likes a controlled lift. Teach a station on a mat by the door so the welcome is a sit, and the bat ears can still take in the whole scene. Life with them is indoor company, a short outing, and a dog who will sulk if left out of the group. That closeness is the behavior to plan for, from puppyhood through the older years. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/french-bulldog/">AKC French Bulldog breed profile</a> records the 1898 recognition and the bat-eared companion outline to see at home.</p>`,
  "guides/french-bulldog-grooming-guide.html": `<p>Fold care is a towel job do with dry hands after dinner. The coat itself is short and smooth, so the work is skin, nails, and the creases a French Bulldog carries on the face and under the tail. Start at the facial fold. A damp cotton pad lifts grit, then a dry cloth follows until the skin feels cool and matte. The tail pocket gets the same pair of cloths. If skip the dry step, the groove is sour by morning.</p>
<p>Bat ears need a look, even though they stand up and seem airy. Fold the leather back and wipe only the part can see. Anything deeper waits for the clinic. Nails on this compact foot click on the floor sooner than people expect, so clip a few at a time while the dog is already on the lap for the fold wipe. A rubber mitt over the back picks up loose hair and lets someone feel for a new red spot.</p>
<p>Baths stay rare. The short coat rinses fast, and dry the face and the rear before the dog heads for the sofa. On day 3 after a bath to recheck every crease, and on day 5 do it again if the weather has been humid. That 3 to 5 day look is the habit, tied to how quickly moisture returns in this skin. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/french-bulldog">VCA remarks on French Bulldog skin folds</a> match the routine to already run at home: clean, dry, and watch the color of the crease.</p>`,
  "guides/french-bulldog-exercise-guide.html": `<p>Monday starts before the pavement holds heat. The French Bulldog wears a harness, the leash clips to that harness, and the neck collar stays home holding tags. People walk one shady block, sniff every post, and turn around while the breathing still sounds like the dog on the couch. Tuesday is indoor. A toy dragged along the rug, then a nap. Wednesday repeats the cool walk if the air is kind, and people skip it if the kitchen already feels warm. This flat chest cools poorly when it pants, so treat warmth as a reason to stop, and heatstroke is the emergency to refuse to practice for.</p>
<p>Stairs are a carry, every day of that week. Pick the dog up at the bottom and put the dog down at the top. The jump off the bed is off the menu. Thursday set a ramp and reward the dog for using it, which spends the brain without spending the back. Friday, if people are near water, a life jacket goes on before any paw tests the edge. These dogs are built close to the ground and they sink in a way that startles people. The jacket is the plan. A pool party is somebody else's sport.</p>
<p>Saturday is a longer sniff in the early light, still in the harness, still short of distress. Sunday is rest, laps, and a chew. Over the registry years, listed 10 to 12, that pattern matters more than any single athletic day. Adults live around weigh 16 to 28 pounds, under the cap of not over 28, and that much dog still has a spine and an airway to protect. Reassess the week's length at month 3 of a new season and again at month 5, when either the heat or the dark has changed the route. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/french-bulldog/">AKC notes on the French Bulldog build</a> describe the compact body to keep comfortable, bat ears and all.</p>`,
  "guides/french-bulldog-puppy-guide.html": `<p>The deposit stays in the account until the parents' papers are on the table. For a French Bulldog litter to want kneecap results, spinal notes, a cardiac comment, eye paperwork, and a breathing grade discussed by name. Whelping often ends in surgery for this breed, so ask how these puppies were delivered and how the dam recovered. A seller who waves that off is telling someone the first year will be a surprise. Rather pay for known films than for a guess.</p>
<p>Growth in year one runs toward a shoulder height of 11 to 13 inches and a weight of not over 28 pounds. The adults around someone finish somewhere in the 16 to 28 pound spread. Weigh the puppy at home each week and write it down, because a cute barrel chest can pass the cap while the bat ears are still the main feature. The short smooth coat shows the ribs if to put a hand on them. By month 3 the nostrils and the palate are part of every vaccine visit. By month 5 want the clinic to listen again, awake, and to watch the puppy move on a nonslip floor.</p>
<p>Stairs are a carry from the first night. The puppy who rockets down steps is practicing a back will pay for later. A ramp to the couch goes in during week one. Harness walks replace neck-collar walks as soon as the chest can hold a strap. Heat rules apply to puppies too, since panting is inefficient and a small body heats fast. Plan the registry window, years 10 to 12, while the dog is still light enough to lift with one arm. Social time is laps, visitors who sit down, and short outings, with sleep taking the larger share of the clock.</p>
<p><a href="https://ofa.org/chic-program/">OFA CHIC program paperwork</a> is the packet have a breeder explain in plain results: what was filed, on which parent, and what the breathing and spine comments actually said. The first year is folds wiped dry, meals measured, and a puppy whose exercise ends before the snore turns harsh.</p>`,
  "guides/french-bulldog-shedding-guide.html": `<p>Black trousers show the shed. French Bulldog hair is short and smooth, and it leaves as a fine dust of straight little spears that stick to fabric, car seats, and the sleeve of a scrub top. There is a single visible coat. See skin when parting it with a thumb. The hair vacuum is that coat letting go of finished shafts, shaft by shaft through every month of the year. Spring adds a bit more. It never arrives as a woolly undercoat dump the way a double-coated dog would fill a rake.</p>
<p>The other place hair collects is wet skin. Facial folds and the tail pocket trap shed shafts against moisture. That mix is how a crease starts to smell. The shed routine is also a dry routine: a rubber curry across the shoulders, then a dry cloth in the fold and the pocket. Loose hair comes off on the curry in a gray film. That pass on day 3 of a humid week and again on day 5 if the dog has been outside. Baths loosen another round, so the towel gets used before the dog hits the couch, or the damp coat prints hair onto the cushion.</p>
<p>Bat ears do not grow a fringe that mats. The leather stays bare, and the shed to find there is just a few short hairs at the base. Color changes nothing about the amount. A brindle and a cream dog leave the same short hair. White fabric hides it. Navy reveals every spear. A sticky roller lives in the work bag because a 10 minute cuddle moves hair onto a black jacket.</p>
<p>Through the years registries list as 10 to 12, the volume stays modest and the locations stay the same: clothes, sofa arms, and creases. Weight still matters to the coat only because a heavier body folds deeper. Grown dogs in the notes sit between 16 and 28 pounds, with the registry cap at not over 28, on a frame 11 to 13 inches tall. More skin roll means more trapped hair. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/french-bulldog">VCA French Bulldog coat description</a> fits what to pull off the mitt: short, smooth, and easy to manage if the folds are dried.</p>`,
  "guides/french-bulldog-lifespan-guide.html": `<p>Published years for French Bulldogs run 10 to 12. Plan a decade and hope for the extra two, and have watched the shorter end arrive when the airway or the back gives out early. The span is a range, a real one, and individual dogs land inside it or slip under it when a crisis hits. The job at home is to stop handing the body extra reasons to quit.</p>
<p>What shortens those years is specific. Pinched nostrils plus a soft palate that hangs long feed brachycephalic obstructive airway syndrome, and panting that moves air poorly turns summer into a heatstroke risk. Hemivertebrae and intervertebral disc disease take mobility and, in the bad weeks, take comfort. Facial-fold and tail-pocket dermatitis become chronic infections if the creases stay wet. Allergies keep the skin angry for years and wear a dog down. A crisis under anesthesia, a litter that needed a cesarean, or a back that was asked to jump every day all sit in the same story of a body with little spare capacity.</p>
<p>Tighten the calendar at year 3 and again at year 5: breathing grade, a watch of the gait on slick floors, a look at patellas, heart, and eyes, and a photo of the folds. Carrying the dog on stairs and using a harness are year-protecting habits, boring and effective. The bat ears and that sleek jacket will look almost unchanged while the inside ages. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/french-bulldog/">AKC French Bulldog published years</a> is where point people who want the 10 to 12 figure from the registry, and then people talk about the airway and the spine that decide whether a given dog reaches it.</p>`,
  "guides/how-big-do-french-bulldogs-get.html": `<p>Shoulder height for a French Bulldog lands between 11 and 13 inches. AKC caps the breed under a 28-pound ceiling. In the clinic the adults to lift usually sit inside 16 to 28 pounds, and a dog who has passed 28 is a dog whose airway and spine are doing overtime. The bat ears add a visual inch the tape does not count. Measure at the withers, where the neck meets the back, with the dog standing square, or the number lies.</p>
<p>Puppies fill early compared with a giant breed, and they can look finished while they are still soft. Weigh at week 8 and again at week 12, then keep the same scale through month 3 and again through month 5. The head and the ears often look adult first. The chest widens after that, and that sleek jacket starts to lie tight over ribs should still be able to find with a flat hand. A puppy who is already 16 pounds has spent a large share of the room under that 28 pound cap. Rather see a lean youngster lengthen than a round one stall near the ceiling of the range.</p>
<p>Males and females overlap more here than in many breeds. The difference visible on paperwork is bone and chest, while the height stays inside 11 to 13. A broad chest changes the harness fit. Refit the strap when the puppy leaves week 12, because the adult chest is wider than the baby one and a loose harness becomes an escape. Stairs stay a carry while that chest is growing. The published size is small enough to lift and heavy enough, at the top end, to matter for a human back too.</p>
<p>People who shop from photos expect a bigger dog. Standing next to a human knee is the real picture: 11 to 13 inches, compact, and done growing in height well before some large breeds start their final fill. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/french-bulldog/">AKC French Bulldog height cap</a> is the line to read with buyers, and then put a hand on the ribs so the 28 pound ceiling stays a ceiling.</p>`,
  "guides/labrador-retriever-health-issues.html": `<p>A young Labrador Retriever who was fetching hard and then folded in the grass is an exercise-induced collapse story until the workup says otherwise. The mutation sits in DNM1. Between episodes the dog looks ordinary, which is why people argue with the diagnosis in the parking lot. What to watch for is weakness that arrives after intense work plus excitement, then a dog who is ready to go again later that afternoon. Quiet neighborhood loops do not provoke it. A frantic retrieve marathon can. Managing it means changing the game, and the clinic visit is how you separate this from heat stress or a seizure.</p>
<p>Hips and elbows are the orthopedic pair to ask about by name. Dysplasia of the hip and dysplasia of the elbow both show up as stiffness after rest, a short stride, or a young dog who bunny-hops into the truck. Include the parents' own hip papers and elbow papers, the kind people file for review, before treating a limp as a simple sprain. Prcd progressive retinal atrophy is the eye disease to mention in the same breath as night hesitation: a Lab that balks at dusk or misses a curb. Centronuclear myopathy is the puppy who looks weak and awkward, with a poor rise from the floor, long before any athletic career starts.</p>
<p>Swimming days have an ear sequel. Dropped ears stay wet, and infections follow. Towel the leather and schedule a recheck if the head keeps shaking. The retrieving mouth is a surgical risk of its own. Socks disappear from the laundry pile. Corn cobs leave the trash and do not stay in the stomach politely. Vomiting, a quiet dog, or a painful abdomen after a counter raid is an emergency, and the object is often one that could have been picked up.</p>
<p>Appetite has a genetic footnote. A 2016 Cell Metabolism paper described a POMC deletion that blunts satiety, and the same deletion is seen in flat-coated retrievers. An empty bowl misleads the scoop. Measure the cup anyway. One full orthopedic and eye review belongs in year 1, and repeat the eye conversation within 2 years if the breeder form is already old. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/labrador-retriever">VCA Labrador Retriever disease list</a> is the handout to keep with those notes.</p>`,
  "guides/labrador-retriever-temperament.html": `<p>Field-bred Labrador Retrievers stay leaner and busier than the broader dogs from show lines. Both are still retrievers. The field dog invents a job at dawn and will carry a bumper until the throwing arm quits. The show dog takes up more sofa and still meets someone at the door with something in the mouth, a slipper if the bumper is missing. That split is the temperament fact to use when someone says all Labs are the same pet. They are the same breed with two common body styles and one obsessive habit: hold an object, deliver it, ask for another throw.</p>
<p>The otter tail is a mood flag. Thick at the base, it sweeps coffee tables clear when the doorbell rings and it goes still when the dog is thinking. Friendly is the default with, including around children who throw a ball badly. The risk is the body behind the friendliness. Females are listed from 55 pounds upward and males can reach 80, so a joyful lean knocks a child down without a shred of meanness. Teach a sit for every greeting while the puppy is still light, and keep that rule when the chest fills in. Visitors get the sit first and the pat second.</p>
<p>Mouth manners are the daily behavior project. A soft retrieving mouth is the ideal, and the real puppy mouths wrists, leashes, and the corner of a rug. Trade the wrist for a toy every single time. Socks and corn cobs are the dangerous version of the same instinct, swallowed because carrying turned into gulping. Counters are scanned. The trash has a lid. A tired Labrador Retriever still picks up whatever a guest dropped, because the work instinct outlasts the workout. Budget 1 pickup lap after visitors leave and a 2 minute check of the yard for tossed food.</p>
<p>Alone time is the hard edge. This is a cooperative gun dog who wants a person in the picture. A crate in the family room, a stuffed chew, and a real outing beforehand beat a long empty day. Field lines feel that emptiness faster. Show lines are calmer in the house and still unravel if the only event of the day is a person coming home. Keep training tiny and frequent: 1 short cue practice after breakfast and another, so 2 touches of work, which spends the mind that might otherwise redesign the shoes.</p>
<p>Water is personality, and so is hunger. They will swim until called, shake on the one person wearing dry clothes, and then stare at the bowl as if the swim erased dinner. The registry year line reads 11 to 14, and have had dogs still bargaining for a retrieve at year 10. The bargain stays fun when ending the game. Colors are only black, yellow, or chocolate, and none of those coats predicts the busy brain. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/labrador-retriever/">AKC Labrador Retriever portrait</a> sketches the friendly sporting dog to recognize, otter tail included, and the weight of that friend is why the sit at the door matters.</p>`,
  "guides/labrador-retriever-grooming-guide.html": `<p>Mud on a Labrador Retriever sits on a short dense double coat that is built to shed water. Rinse at the hose, push the water out with the hands, and towel until the undercoat is damp and the outer coat looks spiked. A bath is for the days the pond smell survives that rinse. Soap goes on, soap comes off, and leave the water out of the ear canal while working. The coat is water-repellent, which means the bath takes longer than the look of the dog suggests, because the undercoat hides dry patches.</p>
<p>Brushing is a weekly job, and it gets heavier when the seasonal shed starts. Use a slicker for the outer jacket and an undercoat rake for the fluff that lifts along the flanks and the pants. One full pass midweek keeps the house reasonable. In the blowing weeks to add a second pass, so the habit becomes 1 to 2 thorough brushes depending on the season. The otter tail needs its own minute. The coat there is thick, and burrs hide at the base where the tail meets the rump.</p>
<p>Ears are the grooming step people skip. After swimming dry the leather, smell the opening, and wipe only the leather a cotton round can reach. A sour smell or a head shake sends that Labrador straight to the clinic, because ear infections after swimming are the pattern to expect in this breed. Nails and the odd sanitary trim under the tail finish the session. There is no feathering to scissor. Clippers are how you ruin the jacket, so leave the coat at the length the water work requires. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/labrador-retriever">VCA Labrador coat and ear care</a> lines up with that weekly rhythm.</p>`,
  "guides/labrador-retriever-exercise-guide.html": `<p>A bumper, a shoreline, and a clock organize the Labrador Retriever's week, because the original job was retrieving from land and from water. Monday is a marked retrieve in the yard: sit, throw, delivery to hand, stop after a handful of reps. Tuesday is a long sniff walk where the nose works and the speed stays easy. Wednesday is swim day if the water has a walk-in exit. Wade with the dog, end the session while the otter tail is still wagging in clean strokes, and dry the ears before people get in the car. Thursday is rest, a chew, and a stroll to the corner.</p>
<p>Friday look at which dog have. Field lines are leaner and busier and will beg for another hundred throws. Show lines are broader and still capable of overdoing a game they love. Exercise-induced collapse, tied to a DNM1 mutation, is why the clock exists. Intense work plus high excitement is the pattern to refuse to train. The rest day is part of the sport. Saturday can be a second swim or a hike with the bumper used only twice. Sunday is family rest and a food-stuffed toy, which spends the retrieving mouth on something that is supposed to be eaten.</p>
<p>Keep 1 harder day and protect it with 2 easy days on either side in a typical week, writing 1 and 2 on the fridge so nobody "just throws the ball" after dinner. Socks get picked up before people leave and again when people return, since a wound-up mouth swallows laundry. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/labrador-retriever">VCA Labrador activity and collapse notes</a> are what to send home with new retriever people who think tired means safe.</p>`,
  "guides/labrador-retriever-puppy-guide.html": `<p>The laundry-basket puppy is aimed at a known adult size, and read that size out loud before any deposit moves. Males finish at 22.5 to 24.5 inches and 65 to 80 pounds. Females finish at 21.5 to 23.5 inches and 55 to 70 pounds. Field-line parents tend to throw leaner, busier pups. Show-line parents throw broader bone. Either way the first year is growth plus a mouth that rehearses the breed's job on the belongings.</p>
<p>Paperwork comes before the check. Ask for hip results and elbow results on both parents, an eye evaluation that speaks to prcd progressive retinal atrophy, and results for exercise-induced collapse and centronuclear myopathy. A breeder who has never heard the DNM1 mutation mentioned is handing someone a research project with a cute face. Ask what the dam and sire eat and whether anyone has mentioned a POMC deletion, the satiety change described by that Cell Metabolism work in 2016 and also seen in flat-coated retrievers. Measured meals start in week one. A puppy who "looks hungry" is behaving normally for this breed.</p>
<p>House life in the first year is a crate long enough for the future otter tail, baby gates, and a floor cleared of socks and corn cobs. Trade mouthing for a toy until it is boring for the puppy and the person. Walks stay moderate while joints are building. Swimming can start in shallow water with someone in the water, followed by an ear wipe. Schedule 1 formal wellness look focused on gait and eyes, and book a 2 month follow-up if anything about the stride is uneven. Roughhousing with big adult dogs waits.</p>
<p>By the end of the year the dog is heavy enough to knock someone off balance and still mentally a puppy. The sit for greetings has to be old news by then. A spare leash hangs by the crate, because the first one gets carried off and punctured before the adult teeth are done. <a href="https://ofa.org/chic-program/">OFA hip and elbow filing</a> is the orthopedic half of the packet to wanted on day one, filed beside the eye and the collapse results.</p>`,
  "guides/labrador-retriever-shedding-guide.html": `<p>The hair on the floor comes out of the undercoat. A Labrador Retriever wears a double jacket, short and dense, outer hair lying close, undercoat soft and thick, the whole jacket water-repellent. Most weeks the shed is a steady sprinkle of short hairs on the baseboards. Twice a year the undercoat lets go and the rake fills in strokes. That seasonal pile is the same coat, just released faster. Weekly brushing is the control. In the heavy weeks to brush, wash the towel, and brush again.</p>
<p>Volume tracks the body. A female near 55 pounds and a male near 80 pounds do not put the same amount of undercoat in the vacuum, and both will surprise a person who thought a short coat meant a clean house. The otter tail sheds too. Its coat is thick and it slaps loose hair onto chairs when the dog is glad to see someone. Comb that base so dead undercoat does not stay packed against the skin.</p>
<p>Color changes where notice the hair, and it changes the biology very little. Black hairs vanish on a dark couch and show on a white shirt. Yellow hairs do the opposite. Chocolate lands everywhere can see. Keep 1 slicker by the door and a 2nd rake in the mudroom during shed season, labeled in the head as tools 1 and 2. After a swim, loose undercoat lifts as the coat dries, so the brush follows the towel. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/labrador-retriever/">AKC description of the Labrador double coat</a> is the standard picture of the jacket under the rake, short and dense and never finished with the job.</p>`,
  "guides/labrador-retriever-lifespan-guide.html": `<p>AKC prints a life line of 11 to 14 years for the Labrador Retriever. Celebrated birthdays inside that window and have lost dogs on the early side when joints or an emergency cut the plan short. The number is a span to organize care around, from the puppy papers through the senior walks that get shorter and more thoughtful.</p>
<p>Dysplasia in the hips and dysplasia in the elbows shorten comfortable years even when they do not end them on a single day. A dog who cannot rise, or who stops wanting the water, loses the life the breed was built to live. Exercise-induced collapse from a DNM1 mutation shortens the safe activity menu. Pushing through it is how a bad afternoon happens. Prcd progressive retinal atrophy takes vision over time. Blind dogs can live well, and they live less well in a house that keeps rearranging furniture. Centronuclear myopathy, when it is severe in a young dog, changes the entire trajectory.</p>
<p>The POMC deletion, the one that blunts satiety and also shows up in flat-coated retrievers, pushes weight onto those joints year after year. Treat the cup as a lifespan tool. A swallowed sock, or a corn cob from the trash, is the sudden version of a shortened life, a surgery or a loss that started as clutter. Chronic ear infections after swimming wear a dog down and sometimes linger into the older years. Plan 1 mobility check each season and 2 senior bloodwork conversations once the dog passes the middle of that 11 to 14 year line. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/labrador-retriever/">AKC Labrador Retriever year span</a> is the figure to quote, and the rest is what to have watched chip away at it. Keeping the cup measured in the senior years protects the same joints that the puppy papers were meant to screen.</p>`,
  "guides/how-big-do-labrador-retrievers-get.html": `<p>A male's shoulder tape reads 22.5 to 24.5 inches, with weight running 65 to 80 pounds. A female's tape reads 21.5 to 23.5 inches, with weight running 55 to 70 pounds. Read both lines to every buyer, because a photo hides 20 pounds and the otter tail makes a youngster look longer than the shoulder tape will confirm. The registry life line beside those figures is 11 to 14 years. Listeners hear people stretch the same aging talk from 10 to 13 when they are guessing, and bring them back to the printed 11 to 14 while size is still the topic.</p>
<p>Field lines finish leaner and busier inside those bands. Show lines finish broader, with more bone and a wider chest, and they can look heavy while still sitting inside the male 65 to 80 or the female 55 to 70. "Broader" describes the silhouette. The scale still sets the cup. Ribs should stay findable under the short dense coat, and a waist should show from above once the puppy fluff settles. The water-repellent jacket hides a thickening middle until the hands go looking.</p>
<p>Filling out takes longer than the height. Measure at month 12, when many of these dogs are near their adult inches and nowhere near their adult substance. The tape returns at month 18, then once more at month 24, because chest, thigh, and the thick base of the otter tail keep developing through that 18 to 24 month stretch. A male who is tall at month 12 and light can still land at 80 pounds later. A female tracking toward 55 can also overshoot 70 if the cup stays at a teenage level. Record weight 1 time a month through year 1, and 2 times a month if the bowl is being "topped off for the growth."</p>
<p>Paws look big early. That is a hint, and the parent's measurements are the better hint. Colors, black or yellow or chocolate, leave the tape untouched. The tailor tape uses a chalk mark on the withers so a crouch cannot steal an inch. Buyers who fall for a loaf at 12 weeks should stand that youngster beside the mother's elbow before they picture an adult male. That tail works as a rudder in the comparison, never as height. The POMC deletion, which dulls the feeling of fullness, can change the scale result without changing the skeleton. A dog can show the right height and the wrong weight for that reason. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/labrador-retriever/">AKC male and female Labrador measurements</a> are the four numbers to write on the puppy folder: two heights, two weights, split by sex.</p>`,
  "guides/golden-retriever-health-issues.html": `<p>Pale gums and a sudden collapse in a middle-aged Golden Retriever put hemangiosarcoma first on the list to hand over. The cancer pattern in this breed includes hemangiosarcoma, lymphoma, and mast cell tumors. Hemangiosarcoma can hide until a bleed, so the signs to tell people to treat as urgent are weakness, a bloated quiet abdomen, white gums, or a dog who was fine at breakfast and cannot stand at noon. Lymphoma often announces itself as lymph nodes under the jaw or behind the knee that feel like firm grapes. Mast cell tumors are skin lumps that swell, shrink, or itch, so note every new lump while it is still small enough to point at. The Morris Animal Foundation runs the Golden Retriever Lifetime Study to follow this pattern across whole lives. The study offers no personal prediction. Use it as the reason a lump gets an appointment this week.</p>
<p>Hearts fail here too, and subaortic stenosis leads the named list along with other heart disease. What to hear in the clinic is a murmur. What people see at home is a dog who tires early on a retrieve, coughs, or faints. Puppies with a murmur get a cardiac workup, and adult dogs who fade on easy ground get one as well. Ask breeders about heart clearances on the parents in the same conversation as orthopedic films. A friendly coat hides a heart problem completely. The dog still wants the bird.</p>
<p>Hips and elbows carry dysplasia in this breed the way they do in other retrievers, with a different daily picture indoors. Morning stiffness, a reluctance to hop into the car, or a front-end limp after a long swim are the clues. Ask for hip radiographs and elbow radiographs, and keep the dog lean enough that a hand can feel ribs under the feathering. Females are often in the 55 pound area and males often top out near 75, so leanness is fingertips on the rib edges, tied to those bands. Extra fat is quiet and expensive.</p>
<p>Ichthyosis shows up on the skin as flakes and scales, sometimes along the belly and trunk, a dull coat that no conditioner fixes. White scale from ichthyosis can dust the table during a brush-out. It is a skin disease to name, and a diagnosis changes the bath product more than it changes the personality. Ears are the other skin-adjacent job. This is a swimmer. Dry the ear openings after every session in the water, because a wet canal is how the head-shaking starts.</p>
<p>The published span is 10 to 12 years, which means the cancer watch and the heart watch run for a long time. Book 1 hands-on lump and node check at home each month, and add a 2nd look in the week after a swim vacation when aming already drying ears and parting feathers. <a href="https://www.morrisanimalfoundation.org/golden-retriever-lifetime-study">Golden Retriever Lifetime Study at the Morris Animal Foundation</a> is the project to name when someone asks why this breed's lumps get more urgency than a casual "watch it."</p>`,
  "guides/golden-retriever-temperament.html": `<p>Scotland built the Golden Retriever as a retrieving gundog, and this dog, out in the yard, still wants the bird. A Golden will mark where a bumper fell in tall grass, hold a sit that wobbles with hope, and deliver to hand with a softness that has to be taught and then protected. The softness is real. So is the enthusiasm that body-slams a guest. Males often land between 65 and 75 pounds and females can start near 55, and that much gladness needs a greeting ritual. Ask for a sit, take the bumper, and then the person gets to say hello.</p>
<p>People are the point of the breed. A golden shut away from the gathering will follow someone from sink to sofa and sigh as if abandoned. With children they are often patient, and the patient dog still mouths sleeves when the retrieve drive has nowhere to go. Give the mouth a legal object and put laundry behind a door. Other dogs are usually welcome. The conflict see is overstimulation, a play session that tips from joy into screaming arousal. Ending the game is a temperament skill, and end it.</p>
<p>Water is emotional. They will wade, swim, and try to carry a stick wider than their chest. Feathering gets soaked and slaps the knees. Plan the mess as part of saying yes to the original job. On dry days, a find-the-bumper game indoors spends the same brain. During the years AKC lists, 10 to 12, the retrieve urge fades in some dogs and stays loud in others. A 10 year old may still negotiate for one more throw. The negotiation stays kind when the rules were built early.</p>
<p>Keep 1 training touch before breakfast and a 2nd in the evening, short enough that people both stay cheerful. The coat color is the famous gold, and the behavior under it is cooperation plus a working memory for where the toy went. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/golden-retriever/">AKC Golden Retriever gundog background</a> is the history to tell people who thought they were bringing home only a couch companion.</p>`,
  "guides/golden-retriever-grooming-guide.html": `<p>The hands find Golden Retriever mats before the eyes do. The coat is a water-repellent double coat with feathering on the ears, the chest, the backs of the legs, and the tail. Behind the ears the hair packs into felt when the brush stays on the easy back. Lift each ear and comb from the skin outward, pinching the base of the mat so the pull stays in dead hair and the skin stays slack. Feathers on the hind legs get a line-brush, a few strands at a time, because a rake dragged through a wet feather rips it.</p>
<p>Swimming is the grooming trap. The jacket sheds water and still holds damp underneath, and the ear canal stays wet after the feathers look fluffy again. Dry ear openings with cotton, then towel the chest and the tail. Burrs ride home in the feathering. Pick them out while the dog is tired and leaning on someone. Nails and the pads get a glance at the same time, since river rock wears nails oddly and sometimes cuts a pad that the long hair hides.</p>
<p>A full brush takes real time on a male near the top of the 65 to 75 pound male band, and it takes less, though the same map, on a female near 55. 1 complete comb-through each week and a 2nd pass on ears and feathers only, which is the 1 to 2 rhythm. Seasonal undercoat release means the comb fills faster and the bath, when bothering with one, needs a full rinse so soap does not stay in the feathers. Clippers belong on a matted emergency, and they are a poor haircut for a coat that is supposed to work in water. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/golden-retriever">VCA Golden Retriever feather and ear notes</a> describe the same places the comb gets stuck.</p>`,
  "guides/golden-retriever-exercise-guide.html": `<p>The original job was a marked retrieve, on land and in the water, and that is the week to build for a Golden Retriever. One day is bumper work in a field: a sit, a throw, a delivery to hand, then a stop while the dog still wants more. Another day is a swim with an easy exit, someone close enough to end it, ears dried before the car. A third day is a long walk where the nose hunts the ditch and the pace stays conversational. The fourth kind of day is rest, because shoulders, hips, and a dog who would retrieve until dark all need a day with no throwing arm.</p>
<p>Write 1 retrieve day and 2 quieter days as the ratio that has kept the dogs sound, so the numbers on the calendar are 1 and 2, adjusted when the weather or the dog's age changes the safe dose. Puppies get the pattern in miniature, with the swim kept shallow. Older dogs keep the pattern and lose the distance. The feathering will be wet on purpose. That is the sport, followed by the towel. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/golden-retriever/">AKC sporting notes for the Golden Retriever</a> are the reminder that this is a gundog schedule, with rest built into it.</p>`,
  "guides/golden-retriever-puppy-guide.html": `<p>Before a deposit, want the parents' story in documents. Hip films, elbow films, and a heart evaluation aimed at subaortic stenosis and other heart disease are the core. Eyes belong on that list too. Question what the seller knows about ichthyosis in the line, because flaky skin is easier to plan for when someone says it out loud. Morris Animal Foundation's Golden Retriever Lifetime Study is underway because cancer, the cluster of hemangiosarcoma plus lymphoma plus mast cell tumors, shapes adult years in this breed. A puppy packet cannot promise a cancer-free life. It can show someone the orthopedic and cardiac work already done.</p>
<p>Size targets sit on the folder. Males grow toward 23 to 24 inches and 65 to 75 pounds. Females grow toward 21.5 to 22.5 inches and 55 to 65 pounds. The outer numbers people quote, 55 to 75, are those sexes laid end to end. Weigh monthly and keep ribs findable under the coming feathering. Those registry years, 10 to 12, are why the puppy cup stays measured. Joints that develop under extra fat are the joints to meet again at year 10.</p>
<p>The first year of behavior is the gundog in miniature. Mouthing, carrying socks, and greeting too hard are the retrieve drive without rules. Trade the mouth for a toy and practice a sit at the door while the puppy can still be moved with one hand. Water play stays wade-deep, with ears dried after. Stairs and slick floors get a rug. Schedule 1 gait-and-heart wellness visit beyond the vaccines, and a 2nd visit if a murmur or a limp was waved off as "puppy." <a href="https://ofa.org/chic-program/">OFA CHIC submissions for Golden Retrievers</a> are the orthopedic papers to expect to see translated into plain language before paying.</p>
<p>Feathering arrives as wispy tufts on the tail and behind the ear flaps, and burrs find those tufts early. A door-frame photo each month shows whether the puppy is tracking the male height or the female height without trusting the fluff. Keep the ramp up and the meals boringly consistent while that photo series runs.</p>`,
  "guides/golden-retriever-shedding-guide.html": `<p>Gold tumbleweeds on the baseboard are two kinds of hair. The Golden Retriever double coat releases undercoat, soft and dense, and the feathering sheds longer strands from the ears, chest, legs, and tail. The undercoat is what packs a rake during the heavy seasons. The feather hairs are what wrap around a vacuum brush and felt into mats behind the ears. If only sweep the floor, miss the mat that is still on the dog, holding dead hair against the skin.</p>
<p>A weekly comb pulls both. When the undercoat releases to comb more often and take the dog outside to do it, because the cloud is real. Males toward 75 pounds and females near 55 produce different volumes and matching strands in different amounts. The water-repellent outer coat does not stop the shed. It hides it until part the feathers. After swimming, drying loosens another round, so the comb follows the towel once the dog is merely damp.</p>
<p>Lived with this shed across a decade registries print as 10 to 12. Gray around the muzzle leaves the shed in place. Home checks are 1 midweek floor sweep that includes the dog, and a 2nd session aimed only at ears and leg feathers, the standing 1 to 2 plan. Clothes, car seats, and the clinic laundry all collect gold. Dark fabric reveals every strand. A comb stays in the glove box. Spring fogs the mudroom windowsill with gold, and bag that hair only after having picked foxtails out of the ear fringe. In winter the quiet shed sneaks into the furnace grate, so the vacuum meets the vent when the heat kicks on. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/golden-retriever">VCA Golden Retriever shedding and coat notes</a> match the places to find hair: undercoat, feathers, and the skin behind the ears. Run a damp hand along the hock feathers after the comb, because those strands cling to socks and get walked through the clinic hallway. The tail plume sheds a second halo onto chair backs when the dog wags at the door.</p>`,
  "guides/golden-retriever-lifespan-guide.html": `<p>Registry years for a Golden Retriever read 10 to 12. Had dogs use all of it, and have had the early years taken by cancer. That span is what to quote. The diseases that cut it are the ones to screen for and the ones to watch with the hands.</p>
<p>Hemangiosarcoma can end a good week without much warning, which is why pale gums, sudden weakness, or a swelling belly are an immediate trip. Lymphoma, together with mast cell tumors, changes the middle years and sometimes takes them. Morris Animal Foundation keeps its Golden Retriever Lifetime Study active, and that project is why the cancer pattern is the longevity question to plan around. Check lumps and nodes as a habit, and keep records of what to felt last month. Heart trouble, subaortic stenosis among the named problems, shortens active years and sometimes end them, especially when a murmur in youth was never imaged. Dysplasia of hips and of elbows removes the hikes and the swims before it removes the dog, and a dog who cannot do the job often ages faster in spirit and in muscle.</p>
<p>Ichthyosis is a skin burden to manage with the right baths. Not claim it sets the year of death. Chronic wet ears, left damp after swimming, become a long irritation that makes older dogs miserable. Weight on a frame whose females start near 55 pounds and whose males reach toward 75 adds load to bad joints. Schedule 1 careful hands-on exam of skin, nodes, and gait each season at home, and make 2 clinic visits a year once people are inside the published span, sooner if a lump appears. A Sunday square on the calendar for feeling nodes and skin keeps the habit off memory alone. Jot size and warmth of anything new. August swims get an ear note in the same book, because a soggy canal and a hidden bleed are different emergencies that both like a distracted summer. <a href="https://www.morrisanimalfoundation.org/golden-retriever-lifetime-study">Morris Animal Foundation lifetime tracking of Goldens</a> is the long project to mention when families ask what actually takes these dogs.</p>`,
  "guides/how-big-do-golden-retrievers-get.html": `<p>For a male to expect height from 23 to 24 inches, with the scale reading 65 to 75 pounds. For a female to expect height from 21.5 to 22.5 inches, with the scale reading 55 to 65 pounds. Laid end to end, the weights people remember are 55 to 75. Both can be correct and they take different space in a corridor. Feathering makes either look larger than the tape. Measure at the withers on a level floor. Ears and skull stay out of the number.</p>
<p>Height arrives before substance. At month 12 see a youngster near the adult inches and still narrow through the chest, with feathering that is only starting to lengthen. Between month 18 and month 24, thigh, chest, and the plume of the tail fill in, and a sloppy cup can push a male past 75 or a female past 65. Water beads on the outer jacket, so visual guesses fail. Hands find the ribs. A waist seen from above is the other check.</p>
<p>Parents predict the landing spot better than a puppy photo does. Two 23 inch parents are a different forecast from a tall male and a small female. The scale happens monthly, through the first year, and on a second date whenever the dog is racing high in the band or stalling while eating well. The gundog outline is athletic, built in Scotland for retrieving, and a wide soft middle is extra mass the joints carry. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/golden-retriever/">AKC Golden Retriever height and weight lines</a> are the measurements to read twice, once for the male line and once for the female line.</p>
<p>Paws look generous early and the chest arrives later, which fools people into buying a tiny harness. Refit the chest strap when the tail feathering lengthens, because that is when the ribcage is spreading. A kitchen doorway makes a decent monthly station: dog square, cloth tape at the withers, scale on the same tile. Write the sex on the card. Blending a 55 pound female target with a 75 pound male target produces one useless number. The Scottish retrieving build is athletic through the loin, and a soft middle is extra mass can feel before the photo shows it.</p>`,
  "guides/german-shepherd-health-issues.html": `<p>Hind toenails worn flat across the top are the early degenerative myelopathy clue in a German Shepherd. The disease is tied to SOD1. At home it looks like a scrape when the dog turns, a sway in the rear, and a reluctance to back up. The dog may show little pain at the start, which is why families wait. Ask whether SOD1 status is known, and ask for a neurologic exam when the nails and the sway show up together. Waiting until the dog falls is waiting too long to name it.</p>
<p>The other mobility file is hips plus elbows. Dysplasia affecting the hips, and the same disease label on the elbows, shows up as trouble rising, a choppy front step, or a youngster who quits play after lying down. Ask for hip images and elbow images on the parents, and image a current dog that is limping too. Big bodies hide a mild limp until the muscle wastes.</p>
<p>Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency is the German Shepherd who eats like a wolf and still loses kilograms of condition, with pale, greasy, cow-pie stool. People think it is a food fad. It is a pancreas that fails to supply enzymes. The fix, once diagnosed, is enzyme on the food and a recheck of body condition. Send stool photos when the appetite and the weight disagree.</p>
<p>Bloat is the night emergency. Restlessness after a meal, unproductive retching, and an abdomen that feels like a drum send someone to the emergency door without a wait-and-see lap. Perianal fistulas are slower and miserable: draining tracts beside the anus, constant licking, pain on sitting, a smell the bath does not clear. Look at that skin on every wellness visit for this breed. Schedule 1 focused hind-end and stool review, and repeat it within 2 months if either the gait or the droppings changed. <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/">Merck Veterinary Manual</a> is where point handlers who need a plain description of gastric dilatation-volvulus before the night they meet it.</p>`,
  "guides/german-shepherd-temperament.html": `<p>A herding dog without an assignment invents one, and the German Shepherd invented door duty. He parked where he could see the walk, greeted people he knew with a lean, and went still and watchful when a stranger reached for the latch. Reserved with strangers is the temperament line to trust. It is a thinking pause. Socializing that pause into a bite is what happens when someone drags the dog forward to "say hi." let him look, reward a glance back at someone, and the stranger waits.</p>
<p>The job can be small and it still has to be real. A place command, a carry of a backpack on a walk, a search for a hidden glove, a heel past distractions: those occupy attention that could otherwise police the household. Without that outlet, this breed can herd children, bark at every passing dog, and patrol the fence line until the grass is a track. The intensity is the working heritage. It needs an outlet with rules, and it needs a person who is interesting enough to follow.</p>
<p>Bonding runs deep and narrow. This dog wanted someone in the room and could take or leave a party. Children in the family were included once they joined the daily routine. Visiting children were data to evaluate. Set up 1 daily working block, even 10 minutes, and a 2nd short practice after dusk so the evening bark had competition. That 1 to 2 rhythm of actual work changed the house more than a longer aimless walk. A bored evening at the window is the behavior to replace with a task the dog can finish. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/german-shepherd-dog/">AKC German Shepherd herding profile</a> describes the versatile working partner to recognize, including the reserve that careless greetings ignore.</p>`,
  "guides/german-shepherd-grooming-guide.html": `<p>Fingers reach German Shepherd skin only after they pass a dense double coat. Part the ruff, the croup, and the pants, and look for redness the fur was hiding. Brushing is a layered job. A slicker skims the outer coat. An undercoat rake pulls the wool that mats against the ribs when shed season starts. Work in sections and keep a hand on the skin so the rake stays honest.</p>
<p>The heavy shed is the point of the grooming week. Outside of those blows, a steady scatter still covers scrubs and car seats. During the blow, rake outside and accept that one session will not finish the dog. Baths are occasional. The coat holds water, so rinse until the water runs clear, towel hard, and let the dog dry fully before the next deep brush, or the next brush works mud into felt. Ears, teeth, and nails ride along. This breed's grooming failure is skipped undercoat, a packed mat along the hindquarters that smells like a wet rug.</p>
<p>Keep 1 rake by the back door all year and bring out tool number 2, a slicker, when the undercoat lifts in sheets, the plain 1 to 2 setup. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/german-shepherd-dog">VCA German Shepherd double coat care</a> fits the routine: get through the coat, get the dead undercoat out, and look at the skin you uncovered.</p>`,
  "guides/german-shepherd-exercise-guide.html": `<p>Mileage without a task leaves a German Shepherd fitter and more frustrated. The week use is built like herding work chopped into house-sized pieces. Monday is heel and a directed carry, a pack or a tug delivered to the hand, with stops. Tuesday is a long decompression walk where sniffing is allowed and pulling is addressed. Wednesday is training: stays, recalls, a search for a glove in the yard. Thursday is easier, a stroll and a chew, because elbows and hips in this breed pay for seven hard days. Friday repeats a job from earlier in the week at a higher distraction, a parking lot or a quiet field. Saturday can be a hike if the dog is adult and sound. Sunday is off, family time, no ball marathon.</p>
<p>Meals and hard running stay apart. Bloat is the emergency this deep chest can meet, so the vigorous day happens well away from the full bowl. Strangers on the route are a temperament drill as much as exercise. Reserved does not mean unsocialized. It means advocate for space and reward attention to someone when someone wants to rush in. Puppies get the same shape of week with the hike removed and the reps tiny.</p>
<p>Mark 1 day as the hardest session and keep 2 days truly easy, with one hard day and two easy days so a bored afternoon does not become an extra sprint. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/german-shepherd-dog/">AKC working notes for the German Shepherd</a> frame this dog as a herder with a job, which is the exercise prescription to follow.</p>`,
  "guides/german-shepherd-puppy-guide.html": `<p>German Shepherd growth is a year-long project with a heavy adult at the end of it. Males are headed for 24 to 26 inches and 65 to 90 pounds. Females are headed for 22 to 24 inches and 50 to 70 pounds. Ask which parents produced this puppy and look at those parents in person if can, because a photo flattens a 90 pound male into a cute head shot. The deposit waits until the health packet matches that future frame.</p>
<p>The packet hold: hip results, elbow results, and a straight answer about SOD1 and degenerative myelopathy. Ask whether exocrine pancreatic insufficiency has shown up in close relatives, and ask about bloat and about perianal fistulas the same way, as family history. Nobody can promise a puppy will avoid those. A seller who will not discuss them is a seller to leave. Orthopedic papers are the part that should already exist on the adults. The rest is honesty.</p>
<p>Homecoming through the first birthday is measured food, controlled play, and a socialization plan that respects a reserved breed. Strangers, slick floors, stairs, car rides, and children are introduced while curiosity still beats suspicion. Let the puppy observe. Reward a check-in. Skip the forced greeting. The herding brain needs a job by the time the teeth are adult: a place to settle, a toy to carry, a short heel. Long runs and weekend hikes wait while the skeleton catches up to the attitude. Jumps off the deck are how elbows get a bad start.</p>
<p>The coat changes during this year too. Puppy fuzz gives way to the dense double coat, and the first real shed startles people who thought the hair was a later problem. Start the rake habit when that undercoat appears. Meals are 1 morning portion and 1 evening portion, and recheck body condition at month 2 so a roly puppy does not become a heavy adolescent. Bloat education is for the humans in the house: know the emergency clinic, know what unproductive retching looks like, and keep wild play away from mealtimes even while the dog is young.</p>
<p>By the second birthday the dog may still be filling chest and thigh. The manners and the stranger skills have to be old by then, because a reserved 70 pound adolescent who was never asked to think is a difficult roommate. <a href="https://ofa.org/chic-program/">OFA CHIC program for German Shepherds</a> is the orthopedic filing to wanted translated, parent by parent, before any money moved. Log weekly weights in a notebook so a sudden jump appears before that chest gets soft.</p>`,
  "guides/german-shepherd-shedding-guide.html": `<p>The heavy shed of a German Shepherd is undercoat releasing from a dense double coat. Outer guard hairs are the sleek layer to see. The wool underneath is what comes out in clumps, packs a rake, and rolls down the hallway like tumbleweed. Twice in the year that release is dramatic. The other weeks are a steady leak of both hair types onto scrubs, onto the car, and onto anyone who sits on the couch after the dog did.</p>
<p>Where it grows matters for where brush. The neck ruff holds a lot of undercoat and sheds it down the chest. The pants and the back of the thighs pack dead wool against the skin if the brush never leaves the topline. The tail throws hair when it wags against a wall. The belly coat is softer and shows up on dark shirts when the dog rolls for a greeting. Part each of those zones with the fingers before deciding the session is done. A shiny back can hide a felted flank.</p>
<p>Tools stay simple and the volume does not. A rake during the blow, a slicker for maintenance, and a bath only when the coat is so full of loose hair that brushing alone smears it around. Rake outdoors in the heavy weeks and vacuum baseboards daily for a fortnight afterward. Living with it means a roller of packing tape in the work locker and a rule that the good black pants stay in the closet on blow days. Skin checks ride along, because perianal irritation and hot spots hide under the same dense coat that produces the shed.</p>
<p>Run 1 full undercoat pass every week in ordinary months, and during the blow that becomes a session every other day plus a 2nd tool in the same evening, so the count to tell new owners is 1 to 2 dedicated sessions depending on the season. Neither session is optional for anyone who intends to see the floor. Shake the crate pad outdoors and wool still comes off the zipper later. Black slacks in a locker tell the truth about this coat. During the big release to budget a porch session with a rake and a grocery bag, then a floor pass after the dog comes inside and rolls. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/german-shepherd-dog">VCA notes on German Shepherd shedding</a> describe this double coat honestly: dense, and serious about the hair it gives up.</p>`,
  "guides/german-shepherd-lifespan-guide.html": `<p>AKC's line for the German Shepherd is often given as 7 to 10 years. Some breed blurbs print 9 to 13. Plan with the shorter line, 7 to 10, and treat 9 and 13 as figures people bring to the clinic from older handouts. Either number is a prompt to talk about what actually removes years, because a dog can miss the low end when bloat or a failing rear end arrives early.</p>
<p>Degenerative myelopathy tied to SOD1 shortens the mobile life and then the life. The scrape of hind toes and the worn top surface of the nails are the start of a long decline. Bad hips and bad elbows do similar work from another direction, taking hikes away and adding pain. Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency, untreated, wastes a dog who is eating plenty. Treated, it can be compatible with a full span, which is why pale greasy stool gets a workup. Bloat can end a night. Perianal fistulas may not set the date of death and they can make the later years miserable enough that families are making quality decisions sooner.</p>
<p>Size is part of the load. Females can be 50 pounds and males can reach 90, and a heavy 90 pound frame on dysplastic hips spends comfort faster than a lean 50 pound one. Start a tighter mobility and weight conversation at year 3 and repeat it at year 5, inside that 7 to 10 window, before the dog is "old" on paper. That thick two-layer jacket, and the heavy shed that comes with it, continue through these years and sometimes hide a thinning rear until the muscle loss is obvious in the bath.</p>
<p>Rehearse 1 bloat plan, the clinic route and the signs, and do a 2nd kind of check each season: toenails, rising, stool, and the skin under the tail. Those two habits, numbered 1 and 2, are the longevity routine, boring and specific. Families who only meet this breed in puppy photos are startled when the mobile years end near the front of that 7 to 10 line. Hearing about SOD1, bloat, and the pancreatic stool story while this shepherd is still trotting makes the later years gentler. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/german-shepherd-dog/">AKC German Shepherd year line</a> is the 7 to 10 figure quote first, with the diseases named right after it.</p>`,
  "guides/how-big-do-german-shepherds-get.html": `<p>The male line on the card says height 24 to 26 inches with a weight of 65 to 90 pounds. The female line says height 22 to 24 inches with a weight of 50 to 70 pounds. The outer weight band mutter when someone says "medium" is 50 to 90 pounds, which is a huge spread and a reason to meet the parents. A 50 pound female and a 90 pound male do not take the same crate, the same car seat, or the same amount of food. Height overlaps in the middle: a tall female at 24 inches and a short male at 24 inches can stand eye to eye and still be built differently through the chest.</p>
<p>Leg length gets a tape at 9 months and at 13 months, with the checkpoint people expect at 12 months. Chest and thigh get a fresh look at 18 months and another at 24 months. Leg length often shows up before the chest and thigh fill. A dog who looks finished at 12 months frequently puts on substance from 18 months into 24 months. That fill is bone and muscle if the work and the cup are right, and it is fat if the cup is a guess. The dense double coat camouflages a thickening waist. Hands go through the coat to the ribs.</p>
<p>Herding condition is the outline inside those numbers: able to move, reserved and athletic, with a job. A soft, inactive dog high in the male range is a different animal from a working dog at the same pounds. Weigh once each month while height is changing fast, or every two weeks if the puppy is a heavy eater or a picky one losing outline. Watch the shoulder angulation as the chest drops, because a pretty stack in a photo can hide a narrow puppy who will still fill the 65 to 90 male line or stay nearer the 50 pound female floor. People also arrive quoting 9 to 13 years beside the size chart. Separate the conversations. Size is the tape and the scale. Years are a different page. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/german-shepherd-dog/">AKC German Shepherd height and weight</a> gives the male line and the female line to copy onto the growth card. Crate length has to include a tail that reaches toward the hock, measured on the adult to expect, or the dog spends nights curled against the door.</p>`,
  "guides/poodle-health-issues.html": `<p>Size is the first question to ask when a Poodle hits the table, because the disease list splits. Standards, the dogs over 15 inches and often 45 to 70 pounds, are where bring up bloat, Addison's disease, sebaceous adenitis, hip dysplasia, and progressive retinal atrophy. Toys and miniatures are where talk about luxating patellas and dental disease, and collapsing trachea belongs in the toy conversation specifically. Miniatures sit above 10 inches and stop at 15. Toy height is 10 inches and under. Mixing those lists wastes the visit.</p>
<p>Bloat in a standard looks like repeated attempts to vomit that produce nothing, a waist that swells, and a dog whose eyes go wide. That is a car-leaves-now emergency. Addison's disease is sneakier. Appetite waxes and wanes, vomiting and diarrhea come and go, and then a dog who seemed to have a stomach bug collapses flat. Electrolytes tell the truth in that crisis, and a known Addisonian needs the replacement plan on the fridge. Sebaceous adenitis shows in the clip: symmetrical hair loss, scale, a coat that goes dull in patches even when the bath routine is faithful. Progressive retinal atrophy shows up as hesitation when the room lights drop, a dog who misses the bottom step at dusk.</p>
<p>Hip trouble in standards is stiffness after a rest or a shorter stride on the hind end. Ask for hip films on the parents and on the adult dog who is already sore. Luxating patellas in toys and miniatures look like a skip, a hind leg held up for several steps, then a return to a normal trot as the kneecap pops back. Dental disease is tartar, odor, and kibble dropped from one side of the mouth. Start looking at toy and miniature teeth early, because the small jaw crowds. Collapsing trachea in toys is a honk when the dog gets excited or when a collar presses the neck. Switch those dogs to a harness and take the honk seriously when it shows up at rest too.</p>
<p>Split the recheck calendar by size. At age 3 and again at age 5, a standard in the care gets an Addison's-aware exam and a hip conversation, while a toy gets a listen to the airway and a dental look, and a miniature gets the patella and the teeth. The hair keeps growing through all of that. Skin disease also becomes obvious on a clip cycle of 4 to 6 weeks. <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/">Merck Veterinary Manual online</a> is the reference to name for bloat so families can read the emergency pattern before they need it.</p>`,
  "guides/poodle-temperament.html": `<p>Water retrieving is the old job, and the non-sporting group is where the registry files the Poodle now. The dog on the chair did not read the group name. A standard will still mark a bumper thrown into a pond. A miniature will carry a sock like it is a duck. A toy will offer a ball with the same serious face. Three sizes, one working brain. Lived with that mismatch between the paperwork group and the actual hobby.</p>
<p>They learn quickly and they remember the loophole. A cue taught sloppily becomes a trick they edit. Keep sessions crisp: a three-minute trick block, then a settle, and later a five-minute practice that ends while they are still correct. That 3 and 5 are minutes, on purpose, because a bored poodle invents a louder hobby. Barking at the window and unpicking the latch are the versions have had to retire. Give the brain a search game or a retrieve and the barking drops.</p>
<p>People are partners in this temperament, close and a bit theatrical. Standards have the leverage to pull an arm off a socket if the leash manners are an afterthought. Toys have the same opinions and less mass, which makes the opinions easier to laugh at and easier to spoil. Teach a default sit for greetings in all three sizes. Clipping the coat changes the silhouette and leaves the personality where it was. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/poodle/">AKC Poodle origin and group placement</a> is the page that holds both facts at once: a water retriever, filed in non-sporting.</p>`,
  "guides/poodle-grooming-guide.html": `<p>Poodle hair keeps growing, so the calendar carries a clip about every 4 to 6 weeks. Low shed still leaves a full grooming job. The coat stays on the dog until a comb or a clipper removes it, and a missed month turns feathers of hair into felt against the skin. Comb to the skin before any clip, ears, armpits, and the tuck-up included. If the comb stops, the clipper is the wrong next step. Mats come out or get clipped off with a guard that protects skin, and then the style can happen.</p>
<p>Feet, face, and the tail need their own attention inside that 4 to 6 week rhythm. Trim the hair between the pads so the foot can feel the floor. Clear the corner of the eyes so tears do not track into a stained rope of hair. The tail tip, if people are keeping length, gets combed most evenings with the fingers during the petting session. Ears get plucked of hair only when the clinic has shown someone that this particular canal needs it. Seen infections started by enthusiastic plucking, so that step stays conservative.</p>
<p>Between appointments comb on a midweek day and repeat if the coat is long. Counting from the clip, do a full comb in week 3 and another in week 5 when the interval is running long, which puts the digits 3 and 5 on the fridge. Standards take longer in the tub. Toys chill faster and get dried promptly. The same rule covers both: dry to the skin, especially the ears. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/poodle">VCA Poodle clipping and coat notes</a> back the schedule already in use, continuous growth and a clip that cannot slip by a month.</p>`,
  "guides/poodle-exercise-guide.html": `<p>A standard Poodle can still do the water work the breed was built for, even though the registry files the breed in the non-sporting group. The week for a standard looks like a retrieve on land, a swim with a sane exit, a training day of tricks and stays, and a day off. The day off is for shoulders and for a deep chest that can bloat, so hard running stays away from mealtimes. Ears get dried when the swim is over. The hair will hold water and the canal will too.</p>
<p>Miniatures get the same pattern with less distance. A retrieve in the yard, a walk with sniffing allowed, a puzzle toy, a rest day. Toys get the brain work at full strength and the mileage cut down: hallway retrieves, a short neighborhood loop, trick practice, and a nap. Collapsing trachea, the toy problem, is a reason the excitement stays moderate and the collar stays off the neck in favor of a harness. Slipping kneecaps in the toy and the miniature are a reason to skip endless pounding on pavement and keep jumps low.</p>
<p>Count laps only to stop. Lap 3 is often where check breathing and joy, and lap 5 is where a toy is usually done even if the standard would continue. Those digits, 3 and 5, keep someone honest with the small dogs. All three sizes quit while the work still looks easy. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/poodle/">AKC Poodle water-retriever history</a> is the justification to give for owning a bumper in a house that also owns clippers.</p>`,
  "guides/poodle-puppy-guide.html": `<p>The deposit sits until know which Poodle is coming home. Standard means over 15 inches, commonly finishing 45 to 70 pounds, and the first year lasts longer in the bone. Miniature covers the band above 10 inches through 15. Toy means 10 inches and under, a dog who can look finished while still needing puppy rules. Parents should match the size class being sold. A "tiny standard" story is how people end up with a dog they did not crate for.</p>
<p>Papers follow the size. For standards want hip results, an eye evaluation that can speak to progressive retinal atrophy, and a plain talk about Addison's disease, sebaceous adenitis, and bloat in the relatives. For toys and miniatures want patellas discussed, and for toys want someone to say collapsing trachea out loud if it has been in the line. Dental disease is common enough in the small sizes that Sellers get questioned about puppy teeth and gums. Nobody hands someone a guarantee. They do hand someone silence or a real answer.</p>
<p>Home life splits by body and stays united by the coat. Hair is already growing and will need a comb and, soon, a clip spaced 4 to 6 weeks apart, scaled to a puppy cut that keeps mats out of the ears and the sanitary area. Brush teeth in the small dogs from the start, a few seconds, because the dental bill is the adult version of skipped puppy gums. Meals are measured. Standards do not need fat piled on growing hips. Toys do not need extra pounds on a kneecap and a trachea.</p>
<p>Training starts as a game and stays a game. Retrieve, settle, a sit for greetings. At month 3 recheck size against the parents so the equipment, the crate, and the expectations match the dog people actually have. At month 5 repeat the tape and book the patella or hip conversation the size class requires. <a href="https://ofa.org/chic-program/">OFA CHIC program for Poodles</a> is the filing language have the seller decode before paying, size class written on the folder in ink.</p>`,
  "guides/poodle-shedding-guide.html": `<p>Loose hair on a Poodle floor is scarce. The coat hair grows continuously, and it leaves when the comb or the clippers take it. What to find in the house is a puff in the comb, a pile under the grooming table, and a clog in the tub drain after a bath. That is the shed, relocated. A low-shed reputation comes from this growth pattern. The hair prefers to stay in the coat until something pulls it free, so missing a clip inside a 4 to 6 week stretch turns length into felt, with no seasonal undercoat dump on the floor.</p>
<p>Saliva still carries allergen. Say that to every allergic visitor before they bury their face in a freshly clipped neck. Licks, chewed toys, and hands that have been "washing" a poodle face can bother a person even when the couch has almost no hair on it. Low shed is a housekeeping fact. Skip describe that fact as an allergy cure.</p>
<p>Where the hair actually comes from is the whole jacket: top of the head, ears, body, legs, tail. Curly length, once it grows out, traps the shed shafts against the skin so you see less on the floor and more in the mat. Run a comb in week 3 after a clip and again in week 5 if the style is being kept long, so 3 and 5 stay on the card. Standards produce a bigger pile in the bin. Toys produce a smaller pile and identical strands in a smaller pile. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/poodle">VCA comments on Poodle hair and saliva</a> belong in the same talk as the clipper schedule.</p>`,
  "guides/poodle-lifespan-guide.html": `<p>Ranges people publish for Poodles often read 12 to 15 years. Groomed dogs at both ends of that pair of numbers, and have seen the early loss when bloat or an Addisonian crisis got there first. The range is a planning figure. What shortens it depends on which size is asleep on the rug.</p>
<p>Standards, commonly 45 to 70 pounds, with some dogs sliding toward 40 when they are ill, lose years to bloat, to Addison's disease, to hip disease, and sometimes to the slow dimming of progressive retinal atrophy that makes an old dog unsafe on stairs. Sebaceous adenitis ruins coat and comfort and can drag a dog's condition down when the skin is severe. A standard who is drifting toward 40 pounds while the bowl is full makes someone think Addison's before thinking "picky." That weight drop inside a breed often discussed from 40 to 70 pounds is a clinical sign in the book, checked with bloodwork.</p>
<p>Toys and miniatures are more often shortened or made miserable by luxating patellas, by dental disease that becomes infection, and, in toys, by collapsing trachea. A honking toy who cannot exercise or sleep is a quality-of-life problem long before a birthday cake. Dental pain stops eating, and not eating changes the later years fast. Clip on a repeating 4 to 6 week gap in old age, because a matted old poodle is an uncomfortable old poodle, and the clip is when finding lumps and skin disease.</p>
<p>When the dog turns 3 and when the dog turns 5 redo the size-specific list: electrolytes and hips and eyes for standards, knees and teeth and airway for the little ones. Those checkpoints, 3 and 5, sit inside a hoped-for 12 to 15. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/poodle">VCA Poodle health and longevity notes</a> are what to hand over with the age plan, size written at the top. A card in the grooming book holds the size class and the birthday. Standards get a line about appetite swings that could be Addison's. Toys get a line about a honk or a skipped hind step. The clip is when old skin tells someone something the walk did not show.</p>`,
  "guides/how-big-do-poodles-get.html": `<p>Three height classes share the Poodle name, and buying the wrong one is the classic size mistake. A standard clears 15 inches and usually lands at 45 to 70 pounds when grown. A miniature is taller than 10 inches and stops by 15 inches. Toy is the class at 10 inches or below. Charts sometimes print a 40 to 70 pound band and a finish window of 10 to 16 months for the middle of the breed. Use the height rules first, because a lean standard can dip toward 40 pounds without becoming a miniature, and a fat miniature can weigh a number that confuses a sloppy chart.</p>
<p>Parents are the forecast. Two toys produce a toy. Two standards produce a standard. A mixed story about "petite standards" needs measurements of the adults, in inches, standing square, hair smoothed down so the clip does not add fictional height. Watched a fluffy puppy get sold as smaller than the bone would allow. The tape goes at the withers. Hair can fake the measurement, and bone cannot.</p>
<p>Filling out follows the class. Toys often look like their adult selves early, and measure during month 3, then once more during month 5, because a growth spurt changes the harness and the crate. Miniatures commonly settle inside that 10 to 16 month talk. Standards keep adding substance after the height is obvious, and a male at the upper end of 45 to 70 pounds needs a crate bought for the adult. The ten-week photo is the wrong crate measurement. The coat grows the whole time and is clipped each 4 to 6 weeks, which changes how big the dog looks without changing the skeleton.</p>
<p>Write three numbers on the folder at purchase: the size class, the parents' heights, and the weight band the standard allows, 45 to 70 for a standard or the lower reality of a toy. Remeasure at the month 3 visit and again at the month 5 visit. The 10 to 16 month window often quoted for the smaller classes gets checked against the actual puppy in front of someone. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/poodle/">AKC Poodle variety size splits</a> are the height divisions to read before believing a listing photo.</p>`,
};
